Everything above this line is `toolkit/lean/AC5.lean` verbatim, followed by
`toolkit/lean/AC3.lean` verbatim except for its `import Mathlib` line and its
header, so the AC certificate verifier of AC3 (`Presentation`, `Move`, `replay`,
`checkCert`, `checkCert_append`, `checkCert_sound`) is available here unchanged.
This section adds the 53 certificates of round 5, direction H4, stored in
`directions/resisters/round4/certs_round5/`:

* `fall_group8.txt`, 8 certificates from the eight resisters of the bound-25 block
  to the standard pair `a,b`;
* `g1_all_pairs.txt`, 28 certificates, all pairs of the bound-24 block of eight;
* `g1_cluster.txt`, 10 certificates, the spanning-tree alternatives of the same
  block of eight (8 of them repeat a pair of `g1_all_pairs.txt` with the same move
  list, 2 are the reverse direction of a listed pair, one of them longer: 206 moves
  against 202), so every piped line shipped in `certs_round5/` is checked here;
* `g5_all_pairs.txt`, 6 certificates, all pairs of the bound-24 block of four;
* `g2_pair.txt`, 1 certificate merging one pair of the block of eight at 111 moves.

Each certificate is replayed by `gen_ac6.py` through `replay_mine.py`, written
from the move definitions, before it is emitted, and is then replayed in the
kernel by `decide` in blocks of at most 12 moves glued with `checkCert_append`.
`<p>_sound` states the Andrews-Curtis equivalence; `<p>_trivial` states that the
start tuple presents the trivial group, for the eight that reach the standard pair.
For the two blocks that round 5 showed to be single classes, `r5g1_m0` to
`r5g1_m7` (and `r5g5_m0` to `r5g5_m3`) name the members, the `_class_` lemmas give
